monster_from:
ZzzCharacter.cpp:13997-14007, CreateMonster, case MONSTER_ICE_QUEEN:

    OpenMonsterModel(MONSTER_MODEL_ICE_QUEEN);
    c = CreateCharacter(Key, MODEL_ICE_QUEEN, PositionX, PositionY);
    c->Weapon[0].Type = MODEL_ANGELIC_STAFF;
    c->Object.BlendMesh = 2;
    c->Object.BlendMeshLight = 1.f;
    c->Object.Scale = 1.1f;
    c->Object.LightEnable = false;
    c->Level = 3;

Scale 1.1 is the only size line. BlendMesh 2 is the glow (glow_from). LightEnable false and Level 3 are the same fact said twice, and neither is a glowing-eye flag: RenderEye is gated on the Bull Fighter's Level 1 alone (ZzzCharacter.cpp:11210). What Level 3 does is in Selection.cpp:100-107 -- SelectCharacter, which runs over every monster on the frames the pointer is over the world, sets `o->LightEnable = true` and then `o->Light` by c->Level, and case 3 is (1.5, 1.5, 1.5), against 0.2 for an ordinary monster and -0.4 for a Level 1 Elite. RenderCharacter draws her at `c->Light = terrain light + o->Light` (ZzzCharacter.cpp:9259), so her body light is at least 1.5 over whatever the ground gives, and MU's fixed-function colour clamps at one: she is drawn at her sheet's own colours, shade or sun, night or day. That is what 'unlit' means for this model, and the client has no way to draw it yet -- see open_question.

attack_skill_from:
OpenMU's Version075 Devias.cs:266: `monster.AttackSkill = ... SkillNumber.PowerWave`, with AttackRange 4 and AttackDelay 1400 in the same block. SkillNumber.PowerWave is 11 and so is MuMain's AT_SKILL_POWERWAVE, so mu.db's attack_skill column (already 11 on row 25) reaches the client's switch untranslated, as the Lich's 2 does.

What it looks like, ZzzCharacter.cpp:5045-5055, in MoveCharacter's AttackTime block (the let-go at `AttackTime >= g_iLimitAttackTime`, 15 frames, :4140 and :172), with Angle[2] aimed at the target by `CreateAngle2D(o->Position, to->Position)` at :4891:

    case AT_SKILL_POWERWAVE:
        if (o->Type == MODEL_ICE_QUEEN)
        {
            Angle[2] += 10.f;  CreateEffect(MODEL_MAGIC2, o->Position, Angle, o->Light);
            Angle[2] -= 20.f;  CreateEffect(MODEL_MAGIC2, o->Position, Angle, o->Light);
            Angle[2] += 10.f;
        }
        CreateEffect(MODEL_MAGIC2, o->Position, Angle, o->Light);
        PlayBuffer(SOUND_MAGIC);

Three of the wizard's waves in a fan, ten degrees either side of the heading, from her own feet, and one SOUND_MAGIC. She is the only monster in 0.75 whose Power Wave is a fan (the Hell Spider's is one). The wave itself is already built -- source/effects/wave/Magic02.json and src/game/fx/wave.h -- and wants only to be cast three times.
